174: ●「迦特臨門」：Gath Rimmon。這名字引起困難，它曾出現在但人分得的城邑當中。可能是阿布澤通遺址(Tell Abu Zeitun)/哲裡舍遺址(Tell Jerishe)。兩個遺址都在今日的特拉維夫附近，距離地中海不超過3 公里，並且很接近雅康河。可能兩地都是這城的遺址。因為古人常常在放棄某個城之後，在附近另建同名的新城。── 約書亞記研經資料(蔡哲民等)
176: 【書二十一24 迦特臨門】按考證這城與兩個相鄰的遺址有關：阿布澤通遺址(Tell Abu Zeitun)和哲裡舍遺址(Tell Jerishe)。兩個遺址都在今日的特拉維夫附近，距離地中海不超過兩哩，並且很接近雅康河。可能兩地都是這城的遺址。因為暫棄城址之後，往往在附近另建同名的新城。此地可能是杜得模斯三世城市名單中，稱為 knt 的迦特；又有可能是亞馬拿版片中提及的吉蒂臨蒙尼(Giti~rimuni)。——華爾頓《約書亞記背景注釋》
===== .\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_21.txt =====
503: Anathoth,
504: Anathoth was a Levitical city allocated to the descendants of Aaron, the priestly line, within the tribe of Benjamin. It is significant in biblical history as the hometown of the prophet Jeremiah (Jeremiah 1:1). The city is mentioned in various contexts, including as a place of refuge and a site of prophetic activity. Anathoth’s association with the priestly class underscores its religious importance. Archaeologically, it is identified with the modern village of Anata, located northeast of Jerusalem. The city’s role in biblical prophecy and its connection to Jeremiah highlight its spiritual significance.
513: Almon was another city assigned to Aaron’s descendants in Benjamin, likely near Anathoth. It is called Alemeth in the parallel Levitical list of 1 Chronicles 6:60. Its inclusion shows that the priesthood was provided with settled communities from which Levites could serve Israel in teaching, worship, and the administration of God’s law.
666: 1Ch 6:69 Aijalon, and Gath-rimmon, together with their pasturelands.
679: Aijalon,
680: Aijalon lay in Dan’s inheritance along a vital valley route between the coastal plain and the hill country. Aijalon means “place of deer.” Joshua’s victory over the Amorite coalition extended toward the Valley of Aijalon, where the moon stood still while the Lord gave Israel victory (Joshua 10:12–14).
682: Jos 19:42 Shaalabbin, Aijalon, Ithlah,
688: and Gath-rimmon—
689: Gath-rimmon was likewise located in Dan’s territory and was assigned to the Kohathite Levites. The name means “winepress of the pomegranate,” suggesting the area’s agricultural productivity. Its place near the western frontier would have situated Levites among communities exposed to Philistine pressure and other influences from the coastal plain.
691: Jos 19:45-46 Jehud, Bene-berak, Gath-rimmon,…
696: Aijalon and Gath-rimmon completed the four cities taken from Daniel for the Kohathite families, together with Eltekeh and Gibbethon. The Levites received no separate tribal territory because the Lord was their inheritance, yet God provided places for their households and ministry throughout Israel.
698: 1Ch 6:69 Aijalon, and Gath-rimmon, together with their pasturelands.
720: they were given Taanach and Gath-rimmon—
721: Taanach stood near important routes in the Jezreel Valley and later figured in Israel’s conflicts with Canaanite powers (Judges 5:19). Gath-rimmon was likewise set apart as a Levitical city. The Kohathite Levites who lived in these towns served Israel in connection with the sanctuary, teaching God’s law and assisting in the ministry appointed to their clan.
725: Jos 19:45 Jehud, Bene-berak, Gath-rimmon,

